WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
INTRO:
After the closing bell on February 1st, 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:09.520
PayPal’s CFO, John Rainey, forecasted that&nbsp;
due to a variety of hurdles and challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.600
investors should be more cautious&nbsp;
about the short-term future of PayPal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.800
Usually, such an announcement may cause a 5 or 7%&nbsp;
decline after hours, but PayPal stuck tumbled 18%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.560
after hours. At this point, executives and&nbsp;
investors likely hoped that a new trading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.480
day would be much kinder to PayPal and that&nbsp;
the fear would subside. But, on February 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.680
losses just continued to compound and PayPal&nbsp;
would end up posting their worst trading day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.480
ever losing a painful 24%. Since then, PayPal&nbsp;
has continued to bleed falling a total of 30%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.320
Now, this decline itself would be considered&nbsp;
a bear market given that the sell-off is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:56.960
than 20%. But this is just the most recent&nbsp;
of PayPal’s losses. Since the end of July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.440
PayPal stock has crashed a total of 60% which&nbsp;
correlates to over a $200 billion loss in market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:11.840
cap. Clearly, PayPal stock is experiencing a&nbsp;
mini depression, so what happened to PayPal?

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.920
BAILED OUT:
Taking a look back, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.960
of PayPal’s biggest shortfalls was that they were&nbsp;
never able to achieve a sustainable operation by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.960
themselves. Like many startups, PayPal had a rough&nbsp;
time turning a profit in the early days. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.240
despite their lack of profitability, they did have&nbsp;
millions of customers thanks to the rapid adoption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.040
of the internet. Focusing on customers as opposed&nbsp;
to profits was perfectly fine throughout the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.200
1990s as it was pretty easy to source funding,&nbsp;
especially for a rapidly growing fintech company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.920
But all of this would change at the turn of the&nbsp;
millennium with the burst of the dot-com bubble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.840
By 2001, it was clear that PayPal needed to&nbsp;
focus on profitability if they wanted to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.840
but their CEO, Elon Musk, seemed to be distracted.&nbsp;
Elon wanted to grow PayPal into a comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.800
financial platform and transition their servers&nbsp;
from Linux to Microsoft. While these ambitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.440
may have been best for the company long-term, the&nbsp;
other executives felt that Elon was forgetting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.720
about the main priority which was turning&nbsp;
a profit. So, while Elon was on vacation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.840
they decided to go ahead and fire him, and Peter&nbsp;
Thiel would takeover. This shifted the company’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.200
focus in the right direction, but they still had&nbsp;
a long way to go. eBay had announced a partnership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.080
with Wells Fargo to launch their own in-house&nbsp;
payment system called Billpoint. And given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.960
61% of PayPal’s revenue came from eBay, this&nbsp;
was not good by any means. In fact, this extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.560
dependence on eBay was the primary reason that&nbsp;
both Google and Yahoo refused to acquire PayPal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.840
The only company willing to acquire PayPal was&nbsp;
eBay, but they had a pretty predatory offer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.680
They offered $400 million and said quote “We are&nbsp;
going to make this offer once and then crush you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.960
you should really take this offer.” Considering&nbsp;
this, Peter decided to throw a hail mary by taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:05.920
PayPal public in February of 2002. Just a few&nbsp;
years ago, this would’ve been easy money, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:11.680
was at the peak of the dot-com crash, so investors&nbsp;
were extremely conservative. Nonetheless, the IPO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:18.320
valued PayPal at $1.2 billion which just goes&nbsp;
to show how predatory eBay’s initial offer was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.840
Seeing this, eBay realized that they had lost the&nbsp;
bidding war, but didn’t want to also lose PayPal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.120
so they offered a much more reasonable $1.5&nbsp;
billion. And given the poor financial state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.160
of PayPal, Peter decided to simply give in and&nbsp;
sell the company. Peter had successfully defended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.520
PayPal from a predatory acquisition, but he didn’t&nbsp;
save PayPal from their dependency issue. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.840
that eBay had control of PayPal, this dependency&nbsp;
would just get worse over the next decade.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.160
GOLDEN CHAINS:&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.800
Even though the acquisition was breeding an&nbsp;
unhealthy dependency on eBay, the acquisition was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.400
quite beneficial in the short term. eBay basically&nbsp;
eliminated PayPal’s profitability issues overnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.280
as they were comfortably able to absorb their&nbsp;
losses. Similarly, now that PayPal and eBay were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.800
no longer competitors, there were no threats of&nbsp;
eBay dropping support for PayPal. So, PayPal could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:14.320
just focus on growth once again, and this was&nbsp;
relatively simple as well. They basically had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.080
entire eBay marketplace to go after, but at the&nbsp;
same time, this heavily limited their potential.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.920
A perfect example of this is the explosive&nbsp;
rise of Venmo. Venmo wasn’t founded till 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.920
or over a decade after PayPal, yet they&nbsp;
dominate the peer-to-peer market today. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.440
Venmo is so popular that Venmo has become a verb&nbsp;
like Google. Another great example of PayPal’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.120
stagnation was Square. Square was founded in&nbsp;
2009 as a simple financial services company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.400
yet they’re worth $60 billion by themselves&nbsp;
today. In both of these cases, you would think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.240
that PayPal had a massive advantage and that they&nbsp;
would’ve crushed Venmo and Square, but under eBay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.920
that wasn’t really possible. Now, this isn’t to&nbsp;
say that PayPal was doing bad. They were rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.960
growing in their own little bubble surpassing&nbsp;
100 million active users in 2011. So, PayPal’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.760
stagnation was more of a lack of expansion as&nbsp;
opposed to a lack of growth in their own niche.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.240
Such expansion efforts were the key to evolving&nbsp;
into a diversified financial services business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.440
but instead, PayPal was stuck under the golden&nbsp;
chains of eBay. But, PayPal wasn’t the only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.400
that was being hindered by the other. You see,&nbsp;
it wasn’t that beneficial for eBay to keep PayPal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.840
around either because there wasn’t much synergy&nbsp;
between the two companies. Sure, PayPal gave eBay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.200
control over payments on the platform, but to get&nbsp;
this control, eBay had to support PayPal in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.960
non-eBay-related business endeavors as well. In&nbsp;
2013, for example, PayPal realized that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.920
losing in the peer-to-peer market, so they decided&nbsp;
to acquire Braintree or Venmo for $800 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.640
This was a great acquisition for PayPal,&nbsp;
but guess who had to pay the bill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.400
Now, it’s not like eBay didn’t want PayPal to&nbsp;
succeed, it’s just that they didn’t have very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.680
in common. For a partnership to work, you need&nbsp;
to be able to share resources and minimize costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.760
For example, Facebook already had a bunch&nbsp;
of app developers and social media experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.640
on board when they bought Instagram. So, the&nbsp;
companies could easily share resources. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.800
it’s not like eBay just had a bunch of financial&nbsp;
analysts and credit card experts laying around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.520
So, from eBay’s perspective, it felt like they&nbsp;
had the burden of running a whole nother company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.280
when all they wanted was an integrated&nbsp;
payment platform. And with time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.000
it became more and more clear that it was&nbsp;
best for both companies if the duo split ways.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.760
ESCAPING EBAY:&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.720
In late 2014, eBay announced that they would be&nbsp;
spinning off PayPal into their own business once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.240
again. The plan was to take the company public&nbsp;
on the Nasdaq, and eBay investors would get 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.880
share of PayPal for every share of eBay they&nbsp;
owned. Aside from this, eBay announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.440
they would continue working with PayPal up until&nbsp;
2020 at which point they’ll switch over to Adyen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:58.400
for payment processing. However, they will still&nbsp;
support PayPal as an option up until July of 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.240
So, PayPal had plenty of time to&nbsp;
grow their business outside of eBay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.080
and this time it was much easier than&nbsp;
their attempt in the early 2000s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.600
PayPal was an established and trusted&nbsp;
brand now which made it much easier for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:15.200
them to garner new online merchants. On top of&nbsp;
this, PayPal was finally profitable as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:19.520
so they didn’t have to constantly worry about&nbsp;
raising more capital. Instead, they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.720
use their profits to buy out other companies,&nbsp;
and that’s exactly what they did. Since 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.840
PayPal has completed a total of 12 acquisitions&nbsp;
with the most famous ones being iZettle and Honey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.960
It wasn’t all smooth though. Right before&nbsp;
PayPal broke off from eBay in mid-2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.360
their draft terms of service got leaked, and one&nbsp;
portion of their TOS especially caught people’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.880
attention. The TOS outlined that PayPal had the&nbsp;
right to robocall and auto-text users at will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.320
and if customers didn’t agree to this, they&nbsp;
were forced to shut down their accounts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.360
To make things worse, PayPal had just paid&nbsp;
$25 million in fines regarding deceptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.800
business practices. So, the last thing they needed&nbsp;
right before their IPO was another PR nightmare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.600
Nonetheless, Paypal did end up working through&nbsp;
these issues, and over the next couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.640
they were able to double their annual income&nbsp;
from about $1 billion to $2 billion. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.200
real growth didn’t come till 2020 when the&nbsp;
pandemic came around. As you would guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.440
when people were locked at home, they were much&nbsp;
more likely to use online-based financial services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.160
as opposed to traditional financial services.&nbsp;
People were also spending much more money online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.080
which just further accelerated PayPal’s&nbsp;
growth. Despite all this growth though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.720
expectations were even higher, and PayPal&nbsp;
was soon not able to live up to the hype.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.400
DISAPPOINTING EXPECTATIONS:&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.880
As soon as the pandemic hit, investors flocked&nbsp;
towards technology stocks. They dumped their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.760
positions in airlines, restaurants, and hotels,&nbsp;
and all this money flooded into tech. This led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.800
to one of the most explosive runs in PayPal’s&nbsp;
history. From their pandemic low, PayPal rocketed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:57.040
244% within the next 12 months. This translated&nbsp;
to a 760% return since IPO or an 8.6X return.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.360
But the problem was that PayPal’s bottom&nbsp;
line had only grown about 5X which meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.240
that PayPal was relatively overvalued&nbsp;
by 72% compared to their IPO valuation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.400
To make things worse, PayPal’s net income was&nbsp;
beginning to stagnate at about $5 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:15.600
And as investors started to realize that&nbsp;
maybe they gave PayPal a bit too much credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.240
and that the stock was relatively overvalued,&nbsp;
they started to sell. This sell-off didn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.360
affect PayPal either. Investors had bid up all&nbsp;
stay-at-home stocks beyond belief, and now they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:31.120
were taking profits left and right. By the start&nbsp;
of 2022, PayPal stock was already down about 40%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.880
and the poor guidance was the nail in the coffin.&nbsp;
Looking back though, PayPals tumultuous history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:42.080
was never their fault. It was almost always&nbsp;
due to their circumstances. Initially, PayPal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.160
struggled to stay afloat in the funding drought&nbsp;
that followed the dot-com bubble. This forced them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.040
to join eBay which largely limited them into&nbsp;
eBay’s marketplace bubble for over a decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.960
After they finally spun off from eBay, they&nbsp;
had to spend years diversifying and building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.080
a business that didn’t solely depend on eBay.&nbsp;
And just as they escaped eBay’s golden chains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:04.080
they got caught up in the tech runup that&nbsp;
followed the covid crash. But, despite all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.120
these setbacks and hurdles, one thing that has&nbsp;
remained constant is PayPal’s resilience, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.560
I don’t think that’s changed. In fact, I think a&nbsp;
lot of investors are sleeping on PayPal right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.240
Just a couple of months ago, PayPal announced&nbsp;
that Amazon would start accepting Venmo in 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:25.120
Now, personally, I don’t think anybody should be&nbsp;
using Venmo to pay for goods on Amazon when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.480
could be earning cashback or rewards using credit&nbsp;
cards. But, regardless of what I think or what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.600
the smartest financial decision, I’m sure there’s&nbsp;
gonna be loads of people using Venmo on Amazon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.520
And even if PayPal only captures a couple&nbsp;
of percent of Amazon’s marketplace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:44.320
we’re talking about tens of billions of dollars&nbsp;
worth of transactions every year. On top of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.480
I think it’s highly likely that people who got&nbsp;
used to PayPal’s services during the pandemic will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.920
just continue to use their services more after the&nbsp;
pandemic whenever that is. So, I think it’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.360
a matter of time until PayPal switches this year&nbsp;
or two of stagnation into a decade of growth. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:04.480
that’s just what I think and it’s not financial&nbsp;
advice. Do you guys use PayPal? And if so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.440
what do you use it for? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you think PayPal’s recovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:13.600
is inevitable. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
out our international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:17.360
in other languages, and consider subscribing&nbsp;
